# Captions and table notes (suggested wording; the captions printed in the paper are authoritative); one entry per delivered item

## Fig. 1 (main text; figures/fig1_protocol.pdf), figures/scripts/fig1_protocol.py (no data)
Caption: The protocol. A design picks n items using only the source models' results; the target model is run on those n items only; an estimator turns its n answers (and the source matrix) into a predicted full-benchmark score, which is compared with the true score. The three splits differ in which models play the target.

## Fig. 2 (main text; figures/fig2_ratio_vs_n.pdf), figures/scripts/fig2_ratio_vs_n.py from results/ratio_by_n.csv.gz
Caption: RMSE of the predicted full score relative to uniform sampling (below 1 is better) against the number of items n, one panel per target split; log-log axes. Lines: geometric mean over non-thin benchmarks (13, 12 for lineage); bands: 95% family-bootstrap interval; one representative per method class plus the three best methods by the primary endpoint (chosen after the results, "post hoc"). In interpolation and lineage all source-aided methods start near half of uniform's RMSE at n = 10 and the ratio rises with n; the IRT-model and anchor plug-in predictors cross 1 at n ≈ 100–400, the difference-corrected methods stay near 0.7–0.8. In extrapolation the IRT-model and anchor plug-in predictors are above 1 from n = 25 and reach 3.8 at n = 400, while the difference-corrected and balanced methods stay at 0.7–0.9.
(Check this sentence against the figure at every re-run: it describes the figure, it is not an input.)

## Fig. 3 (main text; figures/fig3_bias_by_score.pdf), figures/scripts/fig3_bias_by_score.py from results/bias_bands.csv
Caption: Signed error (predicted − true full score, in points) against the target's position, in standard deviations of the source models' full scores above (+) or below (−) their mean; n = 100. Each line is the mean error over the non-thin benchmarks with targets in the bin (targets pooled across benchmarks, so benchmarks with more targets weigh more); the band is its 95% family-bootstrap interval. Bins with fewer than five benchmarks or 30 targets are not drawn. Extrapolation targets lie above every source by construction, so that panel has no bins below +1.
What to see: in extrapolation the signed error of the model-based predictors grows with distance above the sources: uniform × IRT model −5.1, −6.3 and −9.7 points in the bins 1–2, 2–3 and >3 sd, anchor × IRT plug-in −4.9, −7.4, −12.4. The difference estimator reduces this to −0.8, −2.0, −3.2 (uniform × diff. IRT) but does not remove it: its intervals exclude 0. Only the sample-mean designs (uniform, balanced × mean) stay within about 0.7 points of 0. (Numbers read from results/bias_bands.csv.)

## Fig. 4 (main text; figures/fig4_ceiling.pdf), figures/scripts/fig4_ceiling.py from results/ceiling.csv and results/per_bench.csv.gz
Caption: Observed RMSE ratio of uniform × diff. IRT to uniform × mean at n = 100 against the SRS-residual prediction √(1 − q), one point per benchmark and split. q = 1 − Brier(predictor)/Brier(constant predictor) on the unsampled items (fraction of Bernoulli variance explained); left: source item means, no target answers; right: 2D IRT fitted with the target's n answers. The prediction is the ratio reached if the residual sd were the constant predictor's sd times √(1 − q); it is a reference, not a bound. Blue: the three main splits; orange: sensitivity splits (extrapolation by the top 10% and top 50% of models); hollow grey: thin cells.
What to see: points lie below the line in interpolation/lineage and near it in extrapolation (geometric means 0.70 vs 0.81 and 0.854 vs 0.847 over 13 benchmarks); verify against the figure.

## Fig. 5 (appendix; figures/fig5_ablation.pdf; all three splits: fig5_ablation_all_splits.pdf), figures/scripts/fig5_ablation.py from results/primary.csv
Caption: RMSE relative to uniform × mean at n = 100 (geometric mean over non-thin benchmarks) for each design (rows) and estimator (columns); colour is log-scaled and centred at 1 (blue better than uniform, red worse). Boxed italic entries: the 95% family-bootstrap interval includes 1. Blank: combination not run. † IRT-CAT chooses items per target (a different protocol), so its cells are not paired with the shared-subset designs; (uniform × mean is 1 by definition).

## Fig. 6 (appendix; figures/fig6_msrc.pdf), figures/scripts/fig6_msrc.py from results/msrc.csv
Caption: RMSE relative to uniform × mean at n = 100 against the number of source models M (10, 30, 100, all), geometric mean over non-thin benchmarks with a 95% family-bootstrap band; M source models are drawn at random per fold, and a benchmark with fewer than M sources uses all of them (HELM files have about 80 models, so for them M = 100 is all). Designs that use the sources (stratified, balanced, PPS) and estimators that use them are re-fitted at each M; uniform × mean does not use them and is 1 by definition. Anchor selection and info-top were not run for this block.
What to see: with 10 sources the difference estimators are at or above uniform (balanced × diff. IRT 1.10 in interpolation, 1.04 in extrapolation) and fall below it by 30 sources (0.80, 0.86); uniform × IRT model is 1.95 and 3.25 times uniform at 10 sources and 0.78 and 1.96 with all; stratified × mean, which needs sources only for its strata, changes little (0.85 to 0.81 and 0.88 to 0.85). (Numbers read from results/msrc.csv.)

## Fig. 7 (appendix; figures/fig7_ordering.pdf; three splits: fig7_ordering_all_splits.pdf), figures/scripts/fig7_ordering.py from results/pairwise.csv.gz and results/pairwise_bands.csv
Caption: Share of target-model pairs whose order is predicted correctly (ties count half) against the true score gap of the pair, n = 100; pairs pooled over non-thin benchmarks and weighted by pair counts; points are the six gap bins (0–0.5, 0.5–1, 1–2, 2–4, 4–8, > 8 points). Dotted: the sample-only reference, the probability that n uniformly sampled shared items order the pair correctly; a reference for plain sampling, not a bound for source-aided methods. Bands: 95% intervals from a bootstrap over benchmarks (at most 13 units, so coarse), drawn for four methods (uniform × mean, uniform × IRT model, anchor × IRT plug-in, balanced × diff. IRT) because ten overlapping bands cannot be read. IRT-CAT is omitted (items differ per target).

## Table 1 (main text; tables/table1_primary.tex), figures/scripts/tables_results.py from results/primary.csv and results/selected.csv
Title: Primary endpoint: RMSE of the predicted full score relative to uniform random sampling, n = 100 items.
Note: Entry = geometric mean over benchmarks of RMSE / RMSE of uniform × mean on the same targets and draws, [95% family-bootstrap interval; 2000 replicates, target families resampled within each benchmark]. Below 1 is better than uniform. Thin cells (< 10 targets or < 5 families) are excluded; the lineage split is defined for 12 benchmarks (LegalBench has no family information). Methods are design × estimator; the first seven rows are fixed class representatives (one per method class), the last three are the best methods by this endpoint and were chosen after seeing the results, so their intervals and ratios are optimistic. Third block: for each of five method classes, the cell chosen by leave-one-benchmark-out (the cell with the smallest geometric-mean ratio on the other benchmarks of the same split is applied to the held-out benchmark; the row is the geometric mean over held-out benchmarks), which does not use the outcomes of the benchmark it is scored on. Last block: same quantity against stratified × mean as the baseline. The bottom rows count benchmarks, targets and target families, summed over the benchmarks entering. Benchmarks are not independent (HELM models recur across its benchmarks, OLL families across OLL benchmarks); the interval reflects target-family sampling only.
Appendix companions (Tables 5, 7 and 9 below): table5_lobo.tex (per split: the leave-one-benchmark-out cell of each class, its number of distinct choices across held-out benchmarks, ratios against uniform × mean and against stratified × mean, and the post-hoc best of the class against uniform × mean; the post-hoc cell can equal the most frequent leave-one-out cell while the ratios differ, because the leave-one-out cell varies by held-out benchmark); table7_suite.tex (the shown methods for the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM benchmarks separately, against both baselines; longtable); table9_primary_all.tex (every method, non-thin benchmarks; longtable) and table9_primary_allbench.tex (every method, all benchmarks including thin cells; longtable).

## Table 2 (appendix; tables/table2_audit.tex), generated by figures/scripts/table2_audit.py from results/audit.csv
Title: Data audit of the 13 binary score matrices after de-duplication.
Note: Rows are the file names of the data repository (commit 55faff49). Models: rows kept after dropping repeated model names and rows identical to an earlier row ("Dup. dropped" counts both). Families: distinct model families derived from the names by keyword (heuristic); for LegalBench the row-to-model mapping is not published, so families are unknown and every row counts as its own. Constant items: items answered identically by all models (kept). Full score: min / median / max of the models' full-benchmark accuracy. All matrices were checked to be binary.

## Table 3 (appendix; tables/table3_biasvar.tex; _all.tex longtable), figures/scripts/tables_results.py from results/per_bench.csv.gz
Title: Squared bias, variance and mean squared error relative to uniform × mean, n = 100.
Note: Mean over non-thin benchmarks of per-benchmark bias² / MSE(uniform × mean), var / MSE(uniform × mean) and MSE / MSE(uniform × mean). var = mean over targets of the variance of the estimate across subset draws; bias² = mean over targets of the squared draw-mean error minus var / (number of draws), so that bias² + var = MSE; it is an unbiased estimate and can be slightly below 0. Deterministic designs (info-top, anchor medoids) have var = 0 by construction.
R = 100 check (optional sentence; results/highR_vs_main.csv): the table is computed from R = 10 subset draws per target. Recomputing the shared-subset rows with R = 100 draws (interpolation and extrapolation, n = 100) changes every entry shown by at most 0.02, except uniform × IRT model in extrapolation (bias² 4.29 → 4.42, MSE 4.56 → 4.68); lineage and anchor × IRT plug-in were not re-run.

## Table 4 (appendix; tables/table4_ordering.tex; _all.tex longtable), figures/scripts/tables_results.py from results/ordering.csv.gz
