## Table 8 (appendix; tables/table8_robust.tex), figures/scripts/table8_robust.py from results/primary.csv, results/inner50/primary.csv, results/inner50/paired_vs_main.csv and results/kmstrong.csv
Title: Robustness of the difference estimators to the tuning split, and of the anchor design to the k-means.
Note: First block: extrapolation, n = 100, geometric mean over the 13 non-thin benchmarks of RMSE / RMSE of uniform × mean, [95% family-bootstrap interval]. Columns: the shrinkage λ and the other hyper-parameters tuned on the inner split of the main analysis (inner targets = the top 25% of the sources), the same with an inner split of the top 50% of the sources (larger gap between the inner training and inner target models; results/inner50), and λ fixed at 1 (only the IRT difference estimator has this cell; the cell does not depend on the inner split). Last column: paired ratio = geometric mean over benchmarks of RMSE ratio(top-50% inner split) / RMSE ratio(main inner split), with the family resample shared by the two runs (results/inner50/paired_vs_main.csv); below 1 favours the top-50% inner split. The intervals of the first columns are not for comparing the two splits; this column is. Second block: anchor designs on the seven Open LLM Leaderboard benchmarks with the light anchor k-means (the main analysis) and the strong one (10 initialisations, 300 iterations); entry = geometric mean over the 7 benchmarks of each benchmark's RMSE ratio to uniform × mean, with no interval (kmstrong.csv has none); the two columns come from different draws.
What to see: the larger-gap inner split lowers the IRT and kNN difference estimators by 4–6% (paired ratio 0.94–0.96, intervals below 1; uniform × diff. IRT 0.85 → 0.81, balanced × diff. IRT 0.82 → 0.76), leaves uniform × diff. ridge unchanged (0.99 [0.98, 1.01]) and makes balanced × diff. ridge 9% worse (1.09 [1.08, 1.11]; 0.82 → 0.90), so the ordering among difference estimators is not stable to the tuning split; every cell stays below 1. A stronger k-means changes the anchor ratios by at most 0.10 (anchor × diff. IRT in extrapolation 1.21 → 1.11, anchor × mean 1.06 → 1.02) and leaves the pattern: in extrapolation every anchor method except the sample mean stays above 1 (cal. reg. 1.75, IRT plug-in 1.56, diff. IRT 1.11), in interpolation the IRT plug-in stays at 1.14 and the others are within 0.04 of 1. The anchor results therefore do not come from a weak clustering. (Numbers read from the table; check at every re-run.)

