## Table 10 (appendix; tables/table10_recal.tex), figures/scripts/table10_recal.py from results/primary_suite_extra.csv, results/selected_extra.csv and results/recal_fallback_n100.csv
Title: Target-recalibrated estimators and the weak-prior check, n = 100.
Note: Entry = geometric mean over the non-thin benchmarks of the RMSE ratio to uniform × mean (first and last blocks) or to stratified × mean (second block), [95% family-bootstrap interval]; below 1 is better. Recalibrated regression: the target's n answers are regressed on the source-side item score (s: logit of the sources' item accuracy; s + IRT: with the cross-fitted IRT prediction as a second feature), and the fitted values are averaged over all items. Both estimators, the weak-prior rows and the recalibrated class were added after the first results. LOBO = the variant chosen of the eight recalibration variants by leave-one-benchmark-out; † = best of the eight variants per split, chosen after the fact (balanced × recal. (q) in extrapolation, balanced × recal. (s) in the other splits). Diff. IRT (naive) refits without cross-fitting and is a diagnostic. Weak prior: the IRT ability prior precision is 0.1 instead of 1, checked after the fact; the first block of this check shows the default prior. Fall-back rate: share of (draw, target) pairs for which the regression was replaced by the sample mean (fewer than 3 sampled items of one outcome class), pooled over targets and non-thin benchmarks, minimum to maximum over the four designs; it is high only on two benchmarks (openllm_math 48% in interpolation, commonsense 17% in extrapolation). Intervals of rows shared with Table 1 can differ in the last digit (the bootstrap was rerun).
What to see: a recalibrated regression on a uniform design brings it to 0.89 of uniform × mean in extrapolation, which does not reach what stratification alone gives (stratified × mean 0.85), and on stratified or balanced designs it is within ±0.03 of the plain mean (against stratified × mean: 0.95 balanced × mean vs 0.97 balanced × recal. (s) in extrapolation; 0.93 vs 0.90 for the top 10%); it is not better than the naive difference estimator on a balanced design (0.75 in extrapolation). The weak prior lowers the IRT model's extrapolation ratio from 1.96 to 1.66, still above 1. (Numbers read from the files named above.)

## Table 11 (appendix; tables/table11_public.tex), figures/scripts/table11_public.py from results/public_baselines.csv and results/dsweep.csv
Title: Published anchor code on our splits, and a check of our IRT settings, n = 100.
Note: Entry = geometric mean over benchmarks of the RMSE ratio to uniform × mean on the same targets; below 1 is better. One run per cell, so there are no intervals; differences of a few hundredths are not distinguishable. Stage A: 7 benchmarks, extrapolation on one fold, interpolation on five folds; stage B: the 6 benchmarks without MMLU-Pro, one fold per split. "Published code" rows run the public recipe (tinyBenchmarks anchors with the correctness or the IRT weights, Anchor Points, p-IRT on either anchor set; blank = not run in that stage); "ours, same cells" rows are our estimators on the same benchmarks, splits and targets, so their values differ between the stages by the benchmark set. Last block: our IRT model and plug-in on the extrapolation fold of the stage-B benchmarks with 2 or 10 latent dimensions and the default or a wide ability prior (precision 0.01; the weak prior of Table 10 has precision 0.1); the default 2-dimension column is the main run.
What to see: the published recipes with IRT weights (tinyBenchmarks IRT anchors 0.94, p-IRT 1.05 and 0.88 in extrapolation) are close to uniform × mean but not below stratified × mean (0.85), and the correctness-weighted ones are worse (2.49, 1.51 in stage A); in interpolation p-IRT (0.69) is at the level of our anchor × diff. IRT (0.62) and below stratified × mean (0.79). Ten dimensions or a wider prior do not bring our IRT estimators near 1 in extrapolation on an anchor design (1.75–2.10 for the IRT model and plug-in); on a uniform design 10 dimensions are worse (2.00–2.25) and the wide prior slightly better than the default (1.47 vs 1.60 for the plug-in); verify against the table.
